# XSH aio_error()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.aio_error.001
anchor     functions/aio_error.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/aio_error.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For a read of an empty pipe aio_error() returns EINPROGRESS; once
           a byte has been written and the request has completed it returns
           0.
test       t/001-inprogress.c

assertion  XSH.aio_error.002
anchor     functions/aio_error.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For a request that completed successfully aio_error() returns 0,
           and may be called repeatedly before aio_return() with the same
           result.
test       t/002-success.c

assertion  XSH.aio_error.003
anchor     functions/aio_error.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/aio_error.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For an aio_write() to a pipe with no reader that fails, the error
           status is EPIPE (the error write() sets) and aio_return() gives
           -1.
note       A failure already reported by the call leaves no status to
           examine; the test is then UNRESOLVED.
test       t/003-failed.c

assertion  XSH.aio_error.004
anchor     functions/aio_error.html#ERRORS/EINVAL
class      required
since      2001
says       After aio_return() has retrieved a request's status, a further
           aio_error() for the same control block is made; whatever it
           returns is accepted.
note       The page says the call may fail with EINVAL and leaves the
           results undefined for a block not tied to a scheduled
           operation, and an implementation may add errors of its own, so
           nothing about the outcome can be required; the test only checks
           the first retrieval and that the call can be made.
nomutant   the outcome is unspecified; there is nothing to plant
test       t/004-retrieved.c
